WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.760
In 1584, the people of Antwerp made a drastic&nbsp;
decision: they flooded the land surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.720
their city. They resorted to this drastic measure&nbsp;
because the Spanish general Alessandro Farnese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.360
was marching toward them with one goal—he was&nbsp;
determined to conquer Antwerp. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.440
the city was not only a major economic hub&nbsp;
but also the capital of the Dutch Revolt—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.760
war for independence fought by the Dutch&nbsp;
Republic against Spanish rule. For Farnese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.600
stripping the insurgents of this vital stronghold&nbsp;
would be the crowning achievement of a seven-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.640
campaign against the rebellious provinces.
The siege of Antwerp was worthy of an epic film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.880
To starve the city into submission, the&nbsp;
Spanish built a massive bridge across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.680
900-meter-wide Scheldt River, cutting Antwerp off&nbsp;
from the outside world. In a desperate attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.800
to free themselves from this iron grip,&nbsp;
the defenders flooded the entire region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.160
deployed fireships intending to destroy the&nbsp;
bridge, and even built a colossal warship to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.000
break through and reconnect with their allies.&nbsp;
The siege was one of the defining events of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.480
century, and was closely followed by contemporary&nbsp;
observers. Later, even the famous German poet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.960
Friedrich Schiller wrote an account of the events.&nbsp;
This is how modern historiography tells the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:43.253
of the staggering siege of Antwerp.
Chapter 1: The Greatest Prize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.253 --> 00:01:46.000
of the Southern Netherlands
In 1577, Spain received the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.720
shipment of silver that had yet arrived from the&nbsp;
New World—an enormous windfall that filled King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.640
Philip II’s treasury and enabled him to renew his&nbsp;
fight against the Dutch rebels. He entrusted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:08.720
mission to his most capable commander: Alessandro&nbsp;
Farnese, the future Duke of Parma. Parma wasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:15.200
no time. In 1579, he captured the strategic&nbsp;
border city of Maastricht and began securing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.760
control over the southern Netherlands. The rebels&nbsp;
soon found themselves under mounting pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.360
which increased dramatically when&nbsp;
a second front opened in the north.&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.680
In March 1580, George de Lalaing, Count of&nbsp;
Rennenberg, who had long been torn between loyalty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:42.560
to the Spanish crown and sympathy for the rebels,&nbsp;
finally chose sides—aligning himself with Spain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:50.640
As governor of Friesland, Groningen, Overijssel,&nbsp;
Drenthe, and Lingen, his defection carried weight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.640
Most of these territories followed him: only&nbsp;
Friesland and the Ommelanden of Groningen remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:04.720
loyal to the rebellion. The cities of Groningen,&nbsp;
Delfzijl, and Oldenzaal sided with their governor.&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.560
At the same time, King Philip II increased&nbsp;
political pressure. He declared William of Orange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.840
the leader and figurehead of the revolt,&nbsp;
an outlaw. However, this external pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:23.520
had an unintended consequence: it unified the&nbsp;
rebels. On July 26, 1581, the States General of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:30.000
the United Netherlands signed the Plakkaat van&nbsp;
Verlatinghe, in English the Act of Abjuration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.880
formally deposing Philip II as their sovereign.&nbsp;
Today, this document is considered the declaration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.080
of independence of the Dutch Republic.
While the Dutch began to search for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:48.400
powerful patron to support their fight against&nbsp;
Philip II, the Spanish offensive pressed on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:56.080
In October 1583, Lalaing’s successor, Francisco&nbsp;
Verdugo, captured Zutphen. Situated on the IJssel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:03.120
River—the eastern border of the Republic—Zutphen&nbsp;
became a strategic Spanish bridgehead. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.160
Parma’s troops launched thousands of attacks&nbsp;
over the following years. To stop them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.800
William of Orange tried to isolate Zutphen by&nbsp;
establishing a defensive ring of forts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.520
sizable garrisons in Lochem and Dort. For nearly&nbsp;
seven years, this prolonged war of position near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.800
their heartland became the rebels’ primary focus.&nbsp;
Parma exploited their distraction to launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.560
attacks deeper into Flanders and Brabant,&nbsp;
the industrial heart of the Netherlands.&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.760
Parma was a brilliant strategist with a&nbsp;
clear plan: to bring the region to its knees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:42.880
by severing its export routes—and with them, its&nbsp;
economic lifeblood. In 1583, he seized the coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:49.600
of Flanders, then moved northeast to capture&nbsp;
key cities along the Scheldt estuary. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.440
he tightened his grip by sealing off Brussels,&nbsp;
Mechelen, and Ghent before turning his full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:03.360
attention to Antwerp. At the time, Antwerp was the&nbsp;
largest city in the region, home to over 100,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:09.920
people. It was the vibrant economic, financial,&nbsp;
and cultural heart of northwestern Europe—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.160
the capital of the Dutch Revolt. Considered the&nbsp;
greatest prize of the southern Netherlands, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:33.440
fall would be a devastating blow to the Republic.
In early summer 1584, a spy warned William of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.760
Orange that Parma was planning an attack&nbsp;
on Antwerp. William acted immediately. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:46.240
met with the city’s mayor, Filips van Marnix&nbsp;
van Sint-Aldegonde, urging him to immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:52.560
prepare for a siege. William advised breaching&nbsp;
the Kouwensteinse and Blauwgarendijks to flood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.320
a large area north of the city, allowing the&nbsp;
Republic’s fleet to support Antwerp by water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.920
and keep the Spanish from establishing a&nbsp;
foothold nearby. In the meantime, William&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.440
promised to assemble a relief army as quickly&nbsp;
as possible should the city come under siege.&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.760
After the meeting, Aldegonde informed the city&nbsp;
council, stressing that Antwerp’s fate—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:21.360
possibly the Republic’s independence—hung in&nbsp;
the balance. He urged immediate action to carry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.720
out William’s plan. But many council members&nbsp;
doubted Parma could truly threaten the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:33.360
convinced he couldn’t completely cut Antwerp off&nbsp;
from its allies. In addition, the butchers’ guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.320
strongly opposed the plan, fearing the floods&nbsp;
would destroy their most productive pastureland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:44.640
where they fattened around 12,000 oxen each&nbsp;
year. Despite Aldegonde’s urgent warnings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:51.120
the skeptics won out. The council’s only response&nbsp;
was to reinforce the existing defenses along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:58.000
the Scheldt, build a few additional redoubts, and&nbsp;
breach the dikes at Saeftinge to flood that area.&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.400
The preparations were barely complete when a&nbsp;
cavalry vanguard suddenly swept through the area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.280
pillaging as foot soldiers moved to&nbsp;
seize strategic positions. The Spanish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.000
had arrived—Parma was reaching&nbsp;
for the greatest prize of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:27.920
southern Netherlands.
Chapter 2: Surrounded&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:34.960
The main Spanish force reached Antwerp on July&nbsp;
3, 1584. Lacking sufficient troops to launch a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:41.120
direct assault, Parma planned to blockade the city&nbsp;
and starve it into submission. To accomplish this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:48.320
he divided his army into two groups. The first,&nbsp;
led by Count Peter Ernst I von Mansfeld-Vorderort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.520
and Cristóbal de Mondragón, crossed the&nbsp;
Scheldt at Kruibeke to go around the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:08:00.560
while the second targeted Fort Liefkenshoek.
The Spanish first attempted to storm the fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.400
but were repelled by the defenders. Realizing&nbsp;
a second assault would likely fail and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:12.640
securing a position quickly was crucial, they&nbsp;
resorted to a clever ruse. They loaded several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:18.880
wagons with hay and damp grass, set them ablaze,&nbsp;
and pushed them toward the fort’s walls. The thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:25.600
smoke obscured the defenders’ vision, allowing the&nbsp;
attackers to silently scale the walls. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:32.400
the Spanish surged over the ramparts, overwhelming&nbsp;
the defenders and capturing the fort. Parma had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:39.520
successfully completed his first crucial step:&nbsp;
He had gained a foothold just outside the city.&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:51.600
As if the loss of Fort Liefkenshoek weren’t&nbsp;
enough, the Republic suffered an even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:59.120
devastating blow on the very same day: July 10,&nbsp;
1584. The Catholic Balthasar Gérard forced his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:05.760
way into William of Orange’s residence in Delft&nbsp;
and shot him three times at close range. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:13.120
Father of the Republic died almost instantly. The&nbsp;
assassination was a calculated political strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:19.760
long planned and coordinated with Parma.&nbsp;
With William’s death, the rebellion lost its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:26.240
unifying figure. His son and successor, Maurice of&nbsp;
Orange, would one day become a brilliant general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:32.240
but at just 17, he was too young to assume&nbsp;
high command over the forces of the provinces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:38.480
According to historian Olaf van Nimwegen, an&nbsp;
expert on the Dutch revolt, “this left the rebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:45.360
troops without a commander-in-chief.”
Still, the battle for Antwerp pressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.160
on. The Spanish captured Doel, Zwijndrecht,&nbsp;
and Herentals, and by mid-July had established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:55.360
a camp near Stabroek. Soon after, Mondragón&nbsp;
launched an assault on Fort Lillo. As his men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.160
prepared to storm the position, the defenders&nbsp;
accidentally triggered one of their own mines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:06.400
killing dozens of their own troops. But despite&nbsp;
the chaos, Mondragón failed to take the fort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.600
Undeterred by this setback, the Spanish continued&nbsp;
their advance, capturing additional fortifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.400
along the Scheldt and reinforcing the dikes to&nbsp;
prevent the defenders from flooding additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:29.680
flatlands. Step by step, Parma sealed Antwerp off.
Chapter 3: The Unimaginable Becomes Real&nbsp;

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:34.160
Only when the noose was tightening around&nbsp;
their necks did Antwerp’s defenders grasp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.440
the true gravity of their situation. The swift&nbsp;
Spanish advance made it clear that the Duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:45.040
of Parma might accomplish what they believed&nbsp;
impossible. At last, even the butchers’ guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:50.960
relented and agreed to breach the dikes. But&nbsp;
it was too late. The Spanish had already taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:56.320
control of the Kouwensteinsedijk—a critical&nbsp;
position that blocked access to the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:02.720
even if the additional fields were flooded.
The looming blockade—and the suffering it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:09.040
promised—drove many to flee Antwerp. Those&nbsp;
who stayed were left wondering how they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:14.880
provide for themselves. The Spanish had installed&nbsp;
batteries on both banks of the Scheldt, firing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:20.800
any ship that attempted to pass. A few transports&nbsp;
still managed to slip through to the city’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:27.360
harbor, but that lifeline could be severed at&nbsp;
any moment. In response, the city council ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:34.240
every resident to stockpile enough provisions to&nbsp;
last two years. Predictably, the plan failed. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:40.320
wealthy bought little, fearing their stores would&nbsp;
be looted; the poor bought little because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:47.360
couldn’t afford more. Many had always lived hand&nbsp;
to mouth, hardly able to buy food for two days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:54.160
let alone two years. Now they found themselves&nbsp;
completely exposed. The council tried to fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.160 --> 00:12:01.040
the situation by imposing a grain price cap, but&nbsp;
this also backfired. Low prices, combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:06.080
the threat of Spanish cannon fire, discouraged&nbsp;
most captains from risking the journey, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:13.600
Antwerp’s granaries woefully understocked. By&nbsp;
autumn, the situation had become dire. Starvation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:19.280
loomed. The city might not have survived the&nbsp;
winter had a fleet from the allied provinces not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:35.040
broken through with a desperately needed shipment&nbsp;
of grain just before the winter cold set in.&nbsp;

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:40.880
In October, Parma established his headquarters&nbsp;
in Beveren. Strategically, this was an excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:46.720
choice. From there, he could simultaneously&nbsp;
threaten Antwerp, Brussels, Ghent, Dendermonde,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.040
and Mechelen. By now, the blockade of Antwerp was&nbsp;
nearly complete: The city could only be reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:13:01.440
by water, and Parma set out to cut off that&nbsp;
final corridor of entry. His plan seemed insane:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:06.800
The Spanish general intended to build a bridge&nbsp;
across the Scheldt River at a point more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:13.840
900 meters wide. Again, the people of Antwerp&nbsp;
dismissed his idea as impossible. They watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:19.200
in disbelief as hundreds of carpenters and&nbsp;
engineers began constructing forts on both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:25.360
banks and driving heavy pilings into the river&nbsp;
to anchor the bridgeheads. The river’s current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:31.120
made it impossible to span the full width with&nbsp;
fixed structures. To close the gap between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:37.520
bridgeheads, Parma ordered the confiscation of&nbsp;
220 pleiten—flat-bottomed transport ships commonly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:43.200
used on the inland waterways of the region. These&nbsp;
would form a pontoon bridge across the remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:48.960
stretch of water. Work on the massive structure&nbsp;
occupied the Spanish into the early winter. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:55.440
as the river froze over, construction&nbsp;
ground to a halt—quite literally put on ice.&nbsp;

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:01.520
Around the same time, Parma received&nbsp;
intelligence from a spy inside Antwerp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:07.280
tensions were running high, and the city council&nbsp;
had even imprisoned several prominent citizens for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:12.080
attempting to negotiate with the Spanish&nbsp;
without authorization. Hoping to exploit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:17.520
the internal unrest—or, even better,&nbsp;
compel the city to surrender—Parma sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:23.600
a letter to the council on November 13,&nbsp;
offering extraordinarily generous terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:29.360
He promised leniency and protection if the&nbsp;
defenders agreed to capitulate. The people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:36.320
of Antwerp weren’t interested, replying with dry&nbsp;
wit that they, too, were willing to spare him—if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:47.200
he chose to retreat. They had no intention&nbsp;
of surrendering the city without a fight.&nbsp;

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:53.200
As the ice began to melt, the Spanish resumed&nbsp;
construction on the bridge. By late February,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.200 --> 00:15:00.880
a chain of 32 ships stretched nearly 730 meters&nbsp;
between the two fortified bridgeheads. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:07.040
was no ordinary pontoon bridge. The vessels were&nbsp;
lashed together with chains and planks to form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:14.240
the foundation of a floating wall—complete with&nbsp;
a sturdy parapet and artillery emplacements. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:20.080
protect it, two outer rows of boats armed with&nbsp;
sharpened stakes created a floating barricade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:27.920
while a flotilla of 40 warships stood ready along&nbsp;
the banks of the Scheldt. More than 1,500 soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:33.680
manned the structure. The German writer Friedrich&nbsp;
Schiller, who later penned a detailed account of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:40.880
the siege, described the bridge in almost mythical&nbsp;
terms: “This terrible machine was so artfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:47.200
constructed and so richly armed with instruments&nbsp;
of death that it could defend itself like a living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:56.320
creature—spewing fire at a word of command and&nbsp;
pouring destruction on all who came near it.”&nbsp;

00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:02.480
When the defenders realized the Spanish had&nbsp;
actually completed the bridge, disbelief gave way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:18.604
to dread. Antwerp was now cut off entirely. It was&nbsp;
only a matter of time before starvation took hold.&nbsp;

00:16:18.604 --> 00:16:20.560
Chapter 4: The Battle for the Bridge
The people of Antwerp had to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:26.160
free from the Spanish grip—and the sooner,&nbsp;
the better. To reopen access to the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.200
at least for smaller and shallow-draft ships,&nbsp;
they flooded additional land on both sides of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:36.560
the Scheldt. This reduced their dependence on the&nbsp;
river as a sole waterway and forced the Spanish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:42.720
to abandon or reroute along submerged roads.&nbsp;
Several Spanish forts were also flooded—or at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:49.280
least cut off from the rest of their network.
At the same time, the city’s garrison launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.560
frequent raids against the Spanish lines.&nbsp;
These sorties occasionally inflicted losses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.560 --> 00:17:00.160
on the besiegers but caused little lasting&nbsp;
damage: most Spanish positions were heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:04.960
fortified and bristling with artillery, which&nbsp;
the Dutch could not effectively counter from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:10.000
their boats. It was becoming clear that the&nbsp;
only way to loosen the Spanish stranglehold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:24.720
was to break the blockade on the Scheldt.
In mid-March, the defenders received grim news:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:30.080
Brussels, the region’s second large city,&nbsp;
had surrendered. Seizing the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:35.520
Aldegonde intensified his pressure on the&nbsp;
political stage. He urged the States General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:40.400
to speed up their search for an ally and&nbsp;
once again called on the nearby provinces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:47.840
of Zeeland and Holland to send ships to harass the&nbsp;
Spanish and divert their attention from Antwerp.&nbsp;

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:52.720
Meanwhile, the Republic’s fleet had been&nbsp;
far from idle. Since the siege began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:58.720
it had been working to restrict Spanish movements&nbsp;
and keep Antwerp supplied—most notably with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:05.920
crucial grain delivery just before winter. In&nbsp;
early April 1585, Justinus von Nassau sailed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:11.840
the Scheldt with a larger fleet. Within weeks,&nbsp;
they recaptured Fort Liefkenshoek and several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:17.920
other fortresses on Doel Island, securing a&nbsp;
strategic foothold near the Spanish bridge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:23.840
Positioned here, they awaited a signal from&nbsp;
Antwerp’s defenders to launch their attack. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:29.920
plan was to blow up the bridge, split the Spanish&nbsp;
forces, and strike from both sides at once.&nbsp;

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:36.000
Federigo Giambelli, a military engineer from&nbsp;
Mantua who had a score to settle with the Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:42.080
was chosen to take on the bridge. A resourceful&nbsp;
craftsman, Giambelli had previously offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:48.320
his services to Philip II, but the Spanish&nbsp;
king, as historian Christopher Duffy puts it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:54.080
“had little time for such gadgetry … and he used&nbsp;
his courtiers and servants to keep … Federigo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.080 --> 00:19:02.080
Giambelli at an arm’s length.” Frustrated, the&nbsp;
Italian settled in Antwerp, married a local woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:07.840
and earned a living as a physicist and mechanic.&nbsp;
When he learned the Spanish were building their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:14.960
massive bridge, however, the old fire within&nbsp;
him reignited. Giambelli requested two large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:20.320
boats from the city council, loaded each&nbsp;
with around 7,000 pounds of gunpowder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:27.120
and packed them with scrap, rubble, and scrap&nbsp;
iron. Soon, he had his two floating “infernal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:33.680
machines,” as contemporaries called them, ready.
On a starry night, Giambelli lit the fuses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:39.840
set the boats adrift, and let the current&nbsp;
carry them toward Parma’s bridge. One ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:47.360
aground before reaching its target, but the other&nbsp;
struck the base of a bridgehead. A moment of tense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:56.080
silence was shattered by a deafening explosion. A&nbsp;
60-meter-wide gap tore through the structure, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.080 --> 00:20:03.520
the deadly cargo of Giambelli’s fireship rained&nbsp;
for miles around. According to Christopher Duffy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:11.440
the blast killed around 800 Spanish. Parma&nbsp;
himself escaped by the skin of his teeth.&nbsp;

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:17.360
Aldegonde had sent scouts by boat to survey&nbsp;
the scene and assess the damage. But as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:22.480
men cautiously approached the wreckage, cast in&nbsp;
a flickering light by the burning remains of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:29.760
fireships, fear gripped them—another explosion&nbsp;
might still be imminent. Without ever getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:36.560
a clear view of the bridge, they turned back and&nbsp;
reported to Aldegonde that the structure remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:44.320
intact. Disappointed, he called off the operation.&nbsp;
By the time the mayor learned the true extent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.320 --> 00:21:01.715
the destruction, it was too late—Parma had already&nbsp;
reestablished his barricades across the Scheldt.&nbsp;

00:21:01.715 --> 00:21:03.760
Chapter 5: Finis Belli—“The End of War”
In the weeks that followed, the people of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:10.720
Antwerp made several more attempts to blow up the&nbsp;
bridge—always with the same disappointing results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:17.440
They soon realized that breaking the Spanish&nbsp;
blockade this way was impossible and shifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:24.080
their focus to the Kouwensteinsedijk instead.&nbsp;
This nearly four-kilometer-long dam between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:29.840
Stabroek and the Scheldt was the only barrier&nbsp;
preventing allied ships from sailing parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:35.360
to the river through the flooded area to&nbsp;
reach Antwerp. The problem was that Parma had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:42.240
also recognized its strategic importance and had&nbsp;
heavily fortified the narrow strip of land. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:48.000
if the defenders could manage to breach the dike,&nbsp;
Parma’s bridge would be rendered useless—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.000 --> 00:22:02.400
the path to the city would be wide open.
On May 7, the Dutch were ready. Following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:07.040
the plan used in the recent attack on the&nbsp;
bridge, the garrison would make a sortie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:12.000
with three ships and 60 pleiten to push&nbsp;
forward toward the dike while the relief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:18.560
fleet attacked from the opposite side. This time,&nbsp;
the expected signal rocket soared over the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:25.440
and Justinus von Nassau advanced. He landed on&nbsp;
the dike with 500 Zeelanders and immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:31.840
faced stiff resistance. After a fierce and bloody&nbsp;
melee, his men managed to repel the Spanish and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:38.480
began digging in. For a while, they bravely held&nbsp;
their ground against Parma’s superior forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:44.000
but the planned attack from the other side&nbsp;
never came. When the crossfire from the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:49.920
Spanish entrenchments flanking their position&nbsp;
grew more intense, they were forced to retreat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:56.480
The reason for the garrisons’ troops’ failure&nbsp;
to appear remains unclear, but it was likely a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:08.560
simple misunderstanding—a misunderstanding that&nbsp;
cost the lives of dozens of valiant soldiers.&nbsp;

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:13.520
Meanwhile, the people of Antwerp had completed&nbsp;
another ambitious project under Giambelli’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:19.040
supervision. Over the winter and spring, they&nbsp;
had built the largest warship ever launched in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:25.520
Antwerp—a massive floating artillery platform. The&nbsp;
defenders pinned all their hopes on this colossal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:32.880
vessel, christening it Finis Belli, meaning&nbsp;
“End of the War.” Resembling a floating castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:40.240
the Finis Belli was armed with 44 cannons, manned&nbsp;
by 500 musketeers, and fortified with massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:47.600
breastworks and armored crow’s nests, allowing&nbsp;
the crew to fire down on the Spanish positions.&nbsp;

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:53.680
In mid-May, Antwerp’s forces attempted to position&nbsp;
this giant warship near Parma’s bridge to keep it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:59.360
under constant fire. Unfortunately, the floating&nbsp;
platform proved too heavy to maneuver effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:05.440
in the shallow waters of the flooded area. How&nbsp;
exactly the Finis Belli met its end remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:12.240
unclear—some sources say it ran aground when the&nbsp;
tide changed, others claim it hit a dike shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:21.680
after leaving the harbor. Either way, Giambelli’s&nbsp;
second invention also failed to save Antwerp.&nbsp;

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:34.080
On the night of May 26, the Dutch&nbsp;
launched a second amphibious assault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:41.280
on the Kouwensteinsedijk. At dusk, all available&nbsp;
ships left the harbor, while Justinus of Nassau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:46.320
set out with the Count of Hohenlohe and&nbsp;
the relief fleet from the opposite side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:52.960
This time, they threw everything into the&nbsp;
battle—and the coordination finally worked.&nbsp;

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:59.280
The Antwerp forces, led by Aldegonde himself,&nbsp;
began the attack with a ruse. They sent four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:04.800
fireships toward the dike, making the Spanish&nbsp;
believe they were facing floating bombs once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:11.200
again. Haunted by the memory of the attack on&nbsp;
Parmas bridge, the Spanish soldiers immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:18.000
took cover. Too late, they realized it was a&nbsp;
trick. In the brief moments when they ducked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:23.920
behind their parapets, dozens of ships emerged&nbsp;
from the darkness. Seconds later, the Dutch troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:29.120
jumped ashore, erected two redoubts from gabions&nbsp;
they had brought with them, and immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:37.920
began demolishing the dike between them.
The Spanish reacted swiftly. They desperately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:42.800
tried to destroy the makeshift fortifications&nbsp;
and keep the waiting rebel ships at a distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:48.960
with their cannons, but they were overwhelmed by&nbsp;
the unexpected Dutch superiority. The Dutch forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:55.040
pushed them back step by step until suddenly, a&nbsp;
loud cheer erupted—the dike had been breached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.040 --> 00:26:02.160
and the first cargo ship laden with supplies&nbsp;
set course for Antwerp. Hohenlohe and Aldegonde&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:07.280
triumphantly boarded the passing freighter&nbsp;
to proclaim their victory in the city.&nbsp;

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:19.200
However, they soon learned their jubilation was&nbsp;
premature—the Spanish counterattacked. Parma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:24.320
awakened by cannon fire, quickly brought more&nbsp;
artillery into position along the Scheldt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:30.280
advanced against the Dutch. Simultaneously,&nbsp;
Mansfeld pushed onto the dike from the land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:36.640
side. Shortly after, some 5,000 men crowded the&nbsp;
narrow strip of land, engaging in a desperate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:41.600
brutal battle. Soldiers hacked at&nbsp;
each other with entrenching tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:47.920
musket butts, and torches, while the muddy&nbsp;
ground was strewn with the dead and dying.&nbsp;

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:53.600
Gradually, the Spanish pushed the Dutch&nbsp;
back and began to gain the upper hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:59.280
When the tide of the sea finally shifted,&nbsp;
making navigation difficult, the defenders’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:05.520
resistance crumbled. The men of the relief fleet&nbsp;
scrambled to save themselves aboard their ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:13.600
drifting away with the outgoing tide&nbsp;
and leaving Antwerp to its fate.&nbsp;

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:25.120
The defeat at the Kouwensteinsedijk abruptly&nbsp;
ended the garrison’s resistance. From then on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:32.720
with each passing day of the blockade, food grew&nbsp;
scarcer and calls for surrender grew louder—until,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:41.600
on August 17, 1585, Aldegonde finally struck&nbsp;
the flags. Parma’s terms were generous, but all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:48.800
Protestants were forced to leave Antwerp within&nbsp;
four years. This led to massive depopulation, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:57.040
barely 40,000 inhabitants remaining. Antwerp’s&nbsp;
golden era had come to a violent and tragic close.&nbsp;

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:01.440
The conquest of Antwerp was the crowning&nbsp;
achievement of Parma’s offensive. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:07.120
strategic heartland of the Republic was now&nbsp;
encircled—Verdugo had entrenched himself along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:13.440
the IJssel to the east while Parma stood at&nbsp;
the gates of Zeeland in the south. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:19.600
Philip II failed to capitalize on this hard-won&nbsp;
advantage and seize the opportunity to crush the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:27.040
rebellion once and for all. Instead, the Spanish&nbsp;
king dreamed of invading England and ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:32.480
Parma to prepare the southern Netherlands as a&nbsp;
launching point for an attack across the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:39.280
Channel. When the famed Spanish Armada’s invasion&nbsp;
attempt failed in 1588, Philip shifted his focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:46.880
to France, dispatching Parma to prepare the ground&nbsp;
there. Parma spent three years with these futile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:58.000
campaigns before dying weary and frustrated&nbsp;
in Arras on the night of December 2, 1592.&nbsp;

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:04.000
By deploying his forces in campaigns against&nbsp;
England and France, Philip II not only missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:09.680
the chance to reclaim control of the Netherlands&nbsp;
but also gave the rebels time to regroup. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:15.040
these years, Maurice of Orange grew into a&nbsp;
skilled strategist and charismatic leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:23.840
soon ready to lead the reformed armies&nbsp;
of the Republic on to a counteroffensive.

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:28.560
Thanks for watching. If you like to see more&nbsp;
videos on niche topics like the 80 Years War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:33.120
please consider donating on Patreon. We&nbsp;
appreciate every little bit of support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:37.680
a like, a comment, or a donation. So, thank you.

